      "id": 117,
      "level": "intermediate",
      "q": "What is a WebSocket?",
      "a": "The problem before\nThe chat polled /messages every second and the school Wi-Fi drowned. A live score needed the server to shout, but HTTP was one question and one answer. Students opened a raw ws:// in production. CRUD todos moved onto a socket for no reason. There was no long-lived pipe picture versus REST.\n\nWhat this is\nA WebSocket is a long-lived connection so the server can push — chat, live scores, presence. It starts as HTTP, then upgrades (101) to a bidirectional byte/text pipe. After that it is not request/response. Use wss:// (TLS) in production. The browser talks WebSocket; a load balancer must allow the upgrade.\n\nWhat it solves\nThe server can speak first. Ada's phone does not ask \"any news?\" fifty times a minute. Interviews want upgrade, push, and wss. Rooms and fan-out need a pub/sub (Redis) once you have two boxes. Heartbeats detect dead phones. Backpressure matters if you shout faster than Ada can read.\n\nReal-life example\nA walkie-talkie left on between the sports field and the hall. After a short hello on the school phone (HTTP upgrade), both sides can talk without redialing. The score \"2-1\" is shouted to every radio. A letter to the office asking for yesterday's marksheet is still a REST envelope, not a walkie-talkie.\n\nUses\nChat, multiplayer ticks, live dashboards, notifications, collaborative cursors. Amazon asks WebSocket vs HTTP polling vs SSE. Socket.IO adds fallbacks and rooms on top. Nginx must proxy Upgrade headers. Serverless shops may prefer SSE or poll because idle sockets cost. Mobile networks drop sockets; you must reconnect. One socket per tab can add up.\n\nWatch out\nws:// on a public site. No auth on upgrade. Using sockets for GET /todos. Forgetting sticky sessions or pub/sub behind an LB. No heartbeat, so half-dead phones fill the server. Trusting every JSON on the pipe. Huge fan-out from one Node process. XSS in a chat message is still XSS. REST for CRUD, sockets for push — say that split.",
      "code": "const ws = new WebSocket(\"wss://api.example.com/chat\");  // encrypted\nws.onmessage = (e) => console.log(e.data);  // server pushed\nws.send(JSON.stringify({ text: \"hi\" }));",
